+                         blob(p)@HEAD,              crlf_eol(...), normalize_eol(...),
+                         blob(p)@sealed_candidate,  crlf_eol(...), normalize_eol(...) }
+
+* ``MATCH_RAW``         the raw disk bytes hash to ``r``.
+* ``BENIGN_EOL``        some basis ``x`` hashes to ``r`` *and* ``normalize_eol(x) == normalize_eol(d)``
+                        -- a pure checkout / line-ending normalisation, never a failure.
+* ``UNEXPLAINED_DRIFT`` otherwise: the path is missing, no basis hashes to ``r``, or a basis hashes
+                        to ``r`` but its EOL-normalised content differs from the disk content, i.e.
+                        the bytes genuinely changed after the digest was taken.
+
+The content-equality clause is mandatory. Accepting ``blob@candidate`` (or a ``crlf`` variant) on a
+hash match alone is exactly the false pass this gate exists to prevent: on this tree most mismatching
+entries do hash-match the sealed candidate revision, yet 42 of them are real content drift, not a
+checkout artefact. The sealed candidate commit/tree is read from the sibling seal
+(``evidence_manifest.seal.json`` next to the manifest); no candidate is ever hard-coded.
+
+Generation identity: for every manifest entry that is a JSON payload carrying ``candidate_head``, the
+tool records whether that revision is an ancestor of HEAD. This is REPORT-ONLY (never fatal) -- a
+non-ancestor is named in the report but never changes the exit status (a prior adjudication rejected
+making it fatal as a no-op/downgrade).
+
+Exit status: ``0`` by default; with ``--strict``, non-zero iff at least one entry is
+``UNEXPLAINED_DRIFT``. ``--json`` prints exactly
